.\" Manual page for uxsm. Edit this .in file, never the installed page: the
.\" build fills in the version and the binary directory. `make check` lints it.
.\"
.\" Keep this page in step with `uxsm <command> -h`, which is where its wording
.\" comes from, and with docs/architecture.md, which explains the same things
.\" at length.
.TH UXSM 1 "2026-09-26" "uxsm @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uxsm \- X11 session manager for systemd \-\-user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uxsm
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uxsm
starts an X11 session as a set of
.BR "systemd \-\-user" (1)
units: the desktop becomes the main process of a service, the session gets an
environment prepared on purpose, and the standard graphical session targets are
pulled in. When the desktop exits, or the process that opened the session goes
away, systemd stops the whole set and uxsm restores the previous environment.
It is the X11 counterpart of
.BR uwsm (1).
.PP
Once ready, the session activates
.B graphical-session.target
and, by default,
.BR xdg-desktop-autostart.target .
Applications launched with
.B uxsm app
run in separate units inside session-specific slices and stop with the session.
Only one graphical session can use a user's systemd manager and its shared
environment at a time.
.PP
uxsm does not replace the display manager, X server, desktop, or window
manager. It manages the lifetime and environment of the session selected by
the display manager. Most window managers report readiness through EWMH;
desktops that do not can call
.BR "uxsm finalize" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Start an X11 session from a session entry or a command.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the running session.
.TP
.B finalize
Tell uxsm, from the desktop, that the session is ready.
.TP
.B app
Run an application in its own unit, inside the session.
.TP
.B entry
Generate a session entry and install it.
.TP
.B check
Check that the system is ready for uxsm, or whether a session is running.
.TP
.B setup
Change the system so that uxsm works fully.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.PP
Each command takes
.B \-h
for its own options.
.SS start
.SY "uxsm start"
.OP \-e
.OP \-D names
.OP \-\-no-autostart
.I entry.desktop
.YS
.SY "uxsm start"
.OP \-e
.OP \-D names
.OP \-\-no-autostart
.RI [ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.YS
.PP
Start an X11 session and run its desktop as a systemd user service. A single
argument ending in
.B .desktop
is resolved as a session entry in the XDG
.B xsessions
directories. Anything else, and everything after
.BR \-\- ,
is a command whose executable must be in
.BR PATH .
.TP
.BI \-D " names"
Add desktop names to
.BR XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP .
Separate multiple names with a colon.
.TP
.B \-e
Use only the names supplied with
.BR \-D ,
discarding the inherited
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
and the entry's
.BR DesktopNames= .
.TP
.B \-\-no-autostart
Do not start the session's XDG autostart entries. uxsm starts them by default,
as uwsm does.

.SS stop
.SY "uxsm stop"
.YS
.PP
Stop the running uxsm session. This activates the same shutdown path used when
the desktop exits or the display manager's session process disappears.

.SS finalize
.SY "uxsm finalize"
.YS
.PP
Tell uxsm, from the desktop itself, that the session is ready. uxsm also
detects when a window manager takes over the X display; only the first of the
two readiness signals changes the session state.

.SS app
.SY "uxsm app"
.OP \-s slice
.OP \-t scope|service
.OP \-p Key=Value
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ \-\- ]
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.YS
.SY "uxsm app"
.RI [ options ]
.IR entry.desktop [ :action ]
.RI [ "files or URLs..." ]
.YS
.PP
Run an application in its own systemd unit inside the graphical slices of the
session. The unit is visible independently and stops with the session. An
argument ending in
.B .desktop
is an application entry, optionally followed by an action after a colon.
Anything else, and everything after
.BR \-\- ,
is a command.
.TP
.BI \-S " output"
Discard
.BR out ,
.BR err ,
or
.B both
for a service. A scope inherits its caller's output.
.TP
.BI \-a " name"
Set the application component of the generated unit name.
.TP
.BI \-d " description"
Set the unit description.
.TP
.BI \-p " Key=Value"
Pass a systemd unit property in the form accepted by
.BR systemd-run (1),
such as
.BR TimeoutStopSec=5 ,
.BR MemoryMax=2G ,
or
.BR CPUQuota=50% .
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-s " slice"
Select
.B a
for applications (the default),
.B b
for background processes,
.B s
for session components, or give a full name ending in
.BR .slice .
.TP
.BI \-t " type"
Create a
.B scope
(the default) or a
.BR service .
.TP
.BI \-u " unit"
Use this complete unit name instead of generating one.

.SS entry
.SY "uxsm entry"
.RI [ options ]
.I name
.YS
.SY "uxsm entry"
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-exec
.I name
.YS
.SY "uxsm entry"
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-exec \-\-
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.YS
.SY "uxsm entry"
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-plain \-\-from-table
.I name
.YS
.SY "uxsm entry"
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-plain \-\-
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.YS
.PP
Generate a session entry for
.I /usr/local/share/xsessions
and install it only with
.BR \-i .
Without
.BR \-i ,
print the destination and contents without writing anything.
.PP
The default form creates a wrapper named
.B <name>-uxsm.desktop
around an installed
.B <name>.desktop
entry.
With
.BR \-\-exec ,
the generated uxsm entry starts the source command directly. With
.BR \-\-plain ,
the generated entry does not use uxsm. A source is always explicit: an
installed entry, the built-in table requested with
.BR \-\-from-table ,
or a command after
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.BI \-C " comment"
Set the comment shown on the login screen.
.TP
.BI \-D " names"
Add desktop names separated by a colon.
.TP
.BI \-N " name"
Set the name shown on the login screen.
.TP
.B \-e
Use only the names supplied with
.BR \-D ,
discarding names from the source and table.
.TP
.B \-\-exec
Create an uxsm entry that starts the source command directly.
.TP
.B \-f
Overwrite an entry with the same name in
.I /usr/local/share/xsessions
or shadow one in another
.B xsessions
directory.
.TP
.B \-\-from-table
Use uxsm's known desktop table as the source instead of an installed entry.
Requires
.B \-\-exec
or
.BR \-\-plain .
.TP
.B \-i
Write the entry. This requires root privileges.
.TP
.B \-\-no-table
Do not use the known desktop table to fill metadata omitted by the source.
The remaining metadata comes from
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-N ,
and
.BR \-C .
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Create a plain session entry that does not use uxsm.

.SS check
.SY "uxsm check"
.YS
.PP
Run every system check and print an
.BR ok ,
.BR warning ,
or
.B unknown
result. The current check reports whether the active display manager reads the
local session directories where
.B uxsm entry
installs entries.
.PP
.SY "uxsm check is-active"
.OP \-v
.YS
.PP
Report through the exit status whether an uxsm session is running or starting.
With
.BR \-v ,
also print the active units.

.SS setup
.SY "uxsm setup sessions-dir"
.OP \-i
.RI [ lightdm | sddm | gdm ]
.YS
.PP
Make the display manager read the two local session directories
.I /usr/local/share/wayland-sessions
and
.I /usr/local/share/xsessions
where entries installed by hand live. Without an explicit display-manager
name, use the active one. Without
.BR \-i ,
only explain the required change. With
.BR \-i ,
apply it for LightDM or SDDM with root privileges. For GDM, always explain the
required configuration and never modify it.

.SS version
.SY "uxsm version"
.YS
.PP
Print the uxsm version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
Colon-separated desktop names. Without
.BR \-e ,
uxsm combines the inherited value, the session entry's
.BR DesktopNames= ,
and names from
.BR \-D .
.TP
.B XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP
The first desktop name selected for the session.
.TP
.B XDG_SESSION_TYPE
Set to
.BR x11 .
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Contains uxsm's transient state and generated user-manager drop-ins.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
Inherited from the display manager and propagated to the user manager before
the desktop starts.
.PP
The session environment loader reads
.I /etc/profile
and
.I ~/.profile
then loads
.BR uxsm/env ,
.BR uxsm/env-<desktop> ,
and their
.B .d
directories from the XDG configuration and data directories, from lower to
higher priority. On shutdown, uxsm removes session-owned variables and restores
the environment that existed before startup.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/xsessions
Destination used by
.B uxsm entry \-i
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/wayland-sessions
Local Wayland entry directory configured alongside the X11 directory by
.B uxsm setup sessions-dir
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/uxsm
Transient login environment, identity, command, readiness state, and cleanup
records for the current session.
.TP
.I @BINDIR@/../lib/systemd/user
Installed uxsm user-unit templates and application slices.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/uxsm/env
User session environment file.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/uxsm/env-<desktop>
Per-desktop user environment file. Matching files below
.B XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
and
.B XDG_DATA_DIRS
are also loaded, as are the corresponding
.B .d
directories.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded, or help was requested.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime operation failed.
.B uxsm check
also returns 1 when any check reports a warning.
.B uxsm check is-active
returns 1, without printing an error, when no uxsm session is running or
starting.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is invalid. The relevant usage text is printed on standard
error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Start bspwm from its installed session entry:
.PP
.EX
uxsm start bspwm.desktop
.EE
.PP
Start a direct command and set its desktop identity:
.PP
.EX
uxsm start -D bspwm -- bspwm
.EE
.PP
Launch an application in its own unit:
.PP
.EX
uxsm app firefox.desktop:new-private-window
uxsm app -p TimeoutStopSec=5 -- discord
.EE
.PP
Preview and then install a generated session entry:
.PP
.EX
uxsm entry bspwm
sudo uxsm entry -i bspwm
.EE
.PP
Check display-manager configuration and preview its fix:
.PP
.EX
uxsm check
uxsm setup sessions-dir
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uwsm (1),
.BR systemd (1),
.BR "systemd.special" (7),
.BR "systemd\-xdg\-autostart\-generator" (8),
.BR loginctl (1)
.SH AUTHORS
Written by the uxsm authors.
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at https://github.com/heizeisaburou/uxsm/issues
